<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles XI of Sweden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1655">1655</a>–<a href="/years/1697">1697</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Charles XI</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles XI was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the late seventeenth century. He strengthened royal power, reclaimed alienated crown lands from the nobility in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-reduction">Great Reduction</a> and reformed state finances. He also reorganised the army through the allotment system, which provided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> with a sustainable standing army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-xi-of-sweden.jpg" alt="Charles XI of Sweden" width="319" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to David Klöcker Ehrenstrahl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_XI,_1655-1697,_konung_av_Sverige_pfalzgreve_av_Zweibr%C3%BCcken_(David_Kl%C3%B6cker_Ehrenstrahl)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_15083.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born in November 1655, the only son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> and Hedwig Eleonora of Holstein-Gottorp. His father died in February 1660, and a regency led by his mother governed until Charles came of age on 18 December 1672. As an adolescent he devoted himself to sport and bear-hunting and was considered poorly educated. His difficulties are now seen as signs of dyslexia, which was poorly understood then.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> joined the French war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1674, and after the Swedish defeat at Fehrbellin Denmark declared war in September 1675, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scanian-war">Scanian War</a>. Charles took command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>. He won the Battle of Halmstad on 17 August 1676 and fought the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> on 4 December, one of the bloodiest battles of its time, in which about 8,000 of over 20,000 combatants died. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> dictated peace in 1679, which left the Swedish empire practically intact.</p><p>The remaining twenty years of his reign were the longest peacetime of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> of October 1680 he pushed through the reduction, which recovered crown lands and lordships that had been lent or given away. It ruined some nobles and readjusted the nation's finances. The Estates also told him that he was bound by no one but himself, which formally established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>. In 1682 he proposed the army reform in which two farms supported one soldier, and in 1680 he founded the naval base at Karlskrona.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles married Ulrika Eleonora of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> on 6 May 1680. She died in July 1693, and he never fully recovered. He complained of stomach pains from 1694 and died on 5 April 1697, aged 41, of cancer that had spread through his abdomen. Opinion of him has varied, and a 2003 biography by Rystad describes a strong-willed shaper of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> through economic reforms and military strength. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> used the well-trained army he left in battles throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles XI of Sweden” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles X Gustav crossed the frozen belts to force the Treaty of Roskilde on Denmark in 1658, and <b>Charles XI</b>’s allotment system (indelningsverket) settled soldiers on farms that maintained them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The monarchy's ambitions are seen most clearly in <b>Charles XI</b>'s Great Reduction of the 1680s, which returned alienated crown lands from the nobility and greatly increased royal revenues.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick III of Denmark made his crown hereditary in 1660, ending the nobles’ Council of the Realm, and in 1665 signed the King’s Law; <b>Charles XI of Sweden</b> won near-absolute power from the Riksdag in 1680.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>A Swedish army of about 8,000 under <b>Charles XI</b> defeated a Danish army of some 13,000 under Christian V north of Lund in Scania, in the Scanian War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-4">4 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Swedish rule founded schools, including a gymnasium at Reval in 1631, and promoted printing in Estonian; <b>Charles XI's</b> reduction of noble estates in the 1680s angered the nobles.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-estonia-1561-1721">Duchy of Estonia (1561-1721)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles XI's</b> reduction of noble estates provoked the opposition of Johann Reinhold Patkul.</blockquote><a href="/state/swedish-livonia">Swedish Livonia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.33,18.07&amp;year=1682" aria-label="Stockholm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles XI of Sweden"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4483 5011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4483 5011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.33,18.07&amp;year=1682">What was here in 1682? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
